      "qualityReason": "dropped_surname_no_wiki_source"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from the surrounding Palouse region, famous for its rolling wheat and barley fields.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.palousewa.gov/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1888 the Spokane and Palouse Railroad opened, linking Palouse to the Northern Pacific and sparking a boom in lumber and agriculture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historylink.org/File/21016",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Potlatch Lumber Company, a Weyerhaeuser subsidiary, dominated Palouse’s economy in the early 1900s and helped rebuild the town after a fire.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historylink.org/File/21016",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Palouse’s public library opened in 1920 with just 20 donated books, growing into a community hub that still serves residents today.",
